received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/m4gr3d/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"[OpenXR Spatial Containers](https://registry.khronos.org/OpenXR/specs/1.1/html/xrspec.html#XR_EXT_spatial_container) are a new cross-platform spatial windowing API which provides applications the ability to display XR content within bounded or immersive system-managed spaces.\n\nTypically, applications running in fully immersive XR need to run at least at 72fps to provide a pleasant, non-nauseating experience to the user given how much of the user's field of view they occupy.\n\nWhen running in a bounded spatial container however, XR applications take a much smaller field of view, not dissimilar to non-XR, regular flat applications. As such the minimum threshold for a good framerate can be much lower than 72fps.\n\nIn this issue I document some of the Godot spatial container samples running at various framerates below 72fps, and describe my (subjective) experience when interacting with them.","closed_by":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/124106/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/124106/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null}